  "result": "Solving Spatial Supersensing Without Spatial Supersensing (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.16655v1)\nciteturn6744view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn6742view0\",\"lineno\":204}); Total lines: 235\nL204:   * Radford et al. (2021) Alec Radford, Jong Wook Kim, Chris Hallacy, Aditya Ramesh, Gabriel Goh, Sandhini Agarwal, Girish Sastry, Amanda Askell, Pamela Mishkin, Jack Clark, et al. Learning transferable visual models from natural language supervision. In International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML), 2021.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSolving Spatial Supersensing Without Spatial Supersensing (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.16655v1)\nciteturn6744view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn6742view0\",\"pattern\":\"## Appendix\"}); Total lines: 235\nL36: Taken together, our findings suggest that (i) current VSI-Super benchmarks do not yet reliably measure spatial supersensing, and (ii) predictive-sensing inference recipes used by Cambrian-S improve performance by inadvertently exploiting shortcuts rather than from robust spatial supersensing. We include the response from the Cambrian-S authors (in Appendix cite13†A ) to provide a balanced perspective alongside our claims.\nL65: Our two-fold analysis underscores the need for caution when interpreting performance on VSI-Super: rather than reflecting genuine spatial cognition, high scores may simply mean exploiting heuristic shortcuts. We further include a response from the authors (in Appendix cite13†A ) to provide a balanced perspective alongside our claims.\nL66: Overall, our tools – NoSense for VSR and VSC-Repeat for VSC – act as simple stress tests for benchmarks and training methods. We view this kind of meta-evaluation as analogous to ablation studies for models and believe it should be standard for benchmarks.\nL216: ## Appendix A Feedback from Cambrian-S Authors\nL217: \nL218: We aim to foster open discussion in the spirit of journals such as Behavioral and Brain Sciences (BBS), which use an open peer commentary format for controversial work. Before releasing this work, we emailed the Cambrian-S authors to share our findings. We present their response to give readers a more balanced perspective on this work:\nL219: \nL220: Experimental support, please cite56†view the build logs for errors. Generated by cite57†L A T E xml†math.nist.gov .\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEvoVLA: Self-Evolving Vision-Language-Action Model (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.16166v1)\nciteturn6744view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn6742view1\",\"pattern\":\"reward hacking\"}); Total lines: 570\nL164: suppressing spurious progress signals when history context indicates unstable manipulation patterns (e.g., repeated failures, oscillations). We block gradients through $g^{\\text{mem}}_{t-1}$ to prevent reward hacking. Memory update writes back the fused representation $\\tilde{x}_{t}$ only to the selected items $\\mathcal{S}_{t}$, and eviction removes the least useful entry based on a utility score combining usage frequency, recency, and redundancy with existing items.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEvoVLA: Self-Evolving Vision-Language-Action Model (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.16166v1)\nciteturn6744view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn6742view1\",\"pattern\":\"Stage-Aligned\"}); Total lines: 570\nL66: We present EvoVLA, a self-supervised VLA framework addressing this through three synergistic components: Stage-Aligned Reward (SAR) uses triplet contrastive learning with Gemini-generated hard negatives to prevent visual shortcuts; Pose-Based Object Exploration (POE) grounds curiosity in relative object-gripper pose rather than pixels; and Long-Horizon Memory with selective context and gated fusion stabilizes intrinsic shaping.\nL69: cite52†Image: [Uncaptioned image] L70: \nL71: Figure 1: EvoVLA: Addressing stage hallucination in long-horizon manipulation. Our framework combines Stage-Aligned Reward (SAR), Pose-Based Object Exploration (POE), and Long-Horizon Memory to achieve robust performance on complex manipulation tasks. Example results shown: Block Bridge, Stack, and Cup Stacking with Insertion tasks across simulation and real-world deployment.\nL80: Our contributions are threefold. First, we present an SSRL framework that fuses stage-aligned rewards, pose-based curiosity, and counterfactual shaping to suppress hallucination while retaining exploration. Second, we systematically tackle long-horizon memory by combining a context-selected gating module with the Discoverse-L benchmark, enabling both training and evaluation of memory-heavy VLAs.\nL96: EvoVLA addresses both via stage-aligned rewards and pose-grounded SSRL.\nL103: cite95†Image: Refer to caption Figure 3: EvoVLA overview. Built on OpenVLA-OFT backbone, EvoVLA integrates three modules: Stage-Aligned Reward (SAR) with hard negatives and temporal smoothing, Pose-Based Object Exploration (POE) via world models, and Long-Horizon Memory with context selection and gated fusion. The framework couples with Discoverse-L for training and deploys to real robots.\nL109: The architecture centers on three interacting modules. SAR scores image-text triplets with CLIP [cite100†41 ] contrast and temporal smoothing, delivering stage-faithful dense rewards. POE grounds curiosity in relative gripper-to-object poses via paired forward/inverse world models to reduce spurious novelty from visual distractors. Long-Horizon Memory selects salient history tokens via attention-based context selection and gates intrinsic shaping so progress signals remain stable.\nL110: All modules share the OpenVLA-OFT backbone and are optimized jointly via PPO.\nL111: Training follows a modular recipe: optionally warm-start with supervised learning on the rollouts, then apply PPO [cite75†43 ] with task-aligned normalization while SAR, POE, and memory remain active. The following subsections detail each component.\nL112: ### 3.2 Self-Supervised Reinforcement Learning\nL113: \nL114: Our self-supervised learning strategy enables the agent to learn efficiently without manual reward engineering. Following prior work on curiosity-driven exploration [cite63†38 , cite64†7 ], we generate intrinsic rewards from pretext tasks. However, we ground these signals in stage-aligned evaluation and geometric structure rather than raw pixels, yielding more reliable learning signals. The combined reward is:\nL115:  | $$\\tilde{r}_{t}=r^{e}_{t}+\\rho\\big(r^{\\text{stage}}_{t}+r^{\\text{cur}}_{t}+r^{\\text{prog}}_{t}\\big).$$  |  | (1)\nL116: \nL117: where $r^{e}_{t}$ is the sparse external task reward, $r^{\\text{stage}}_{t}$ is the stage-aligned reward, $r^{\\text{cur}}_{t}$ stems from POE, $r^{\\text{prog}}_{t}$ comes from Long-Horizon Memory, and $\\rho$ is the fixed intrinsic weight (we use $\\rho=0.6$, selected via sensitivity analysis; Supp. Mat. Fig. cite101†10 ). Below, we detail each component.\nL118: Stage-Aligned Reward. We evaluate stage completion through image-text contrastive learning, producing dense reward signals to guide policy learning. The key insight is that standard positive-negative contrastive learning is insufficient, so we need hard negatives that capture near-miss states to prevent policies from exploiting visual shortcuts.\nL119: Triplet text generation. For each stage $k$, the Gemini 2.5 Pro [cite99†13 ] stage dictionary provides a triplet: positive $T^{+}_{k}$ (completion state), mutually-exclusive negative $T^{-}_{k}$, and counterfactual hard negative $T^{h-}_{k}$ (near-miss). The hard negative uses spatial/contact predicates (e.g., “grasping non-target object”) rather than visual features (e.g., “grasping blue cup”), ensuring robustness to appearance variations.\nL120: Image-text contrastive scoring. Given VLM encoder $\\phi$ (CLIP [cite100†41 , cite102†20 , cite103†21 ]), we compute stage-aligned scores by comparing image observations against the triplet text descriptions:\nL121: \nL122:  | $\\displaystyle u_{k}(t)$  | $\\displaystyle=\\sigma\\Big(\\tau\\,[s^{+}_{k}(t)-\\max\\{s^{-}_{k}(t),s^{h}_{k}(t)\\}]\\Big),$  |  | (2)\nL123:  | $\\displaystyle u_{k}(t)$  | $\\displaystyle\\in[0,1],$  |\nL124: where $s^{+}_{k}(t)=\\langle\\phi_{\\text{img}}(o_{t}),\\phi_{\\text{text}}(T^{+}_{k})\\rangle$ (similarly for $s^{-}_{k}(t),s^{h}_{k}(t)$), $\\tau$ is the temperature parameter, and $\\sigma$ is the sigmoid function. This contrastive formulation forces the VLM to distinguish between near-miss states and actual completion.\nL125: \nL126: Temporal smoothing. To stabilize score fluctuations and prevent noisy reward signals, we maintain a running average $\\bar{u}_{k}$ that is updated smoothly over time:\nL127:  | $$\\bar{u}_{k}(t)=(1-\\alpha)\\bar{u}_{k}(t-1)+\\alpha u_{k}(t).$$  |  | (3)\nL128: \nL129: where $\\alpha$ is the smoothing coefficient. The stage reward is then computed as the progress in these smoothed scores:\nL130: \nL131:  | $$r^{\\text{stage}}_{t}=\\bar{u}_{\\kappa_{t}}(t)-\\bar{u}_{\\kappa_{t}}(t-1).$$  |  | (4)\nL227: These observations align with the quantitative Stack gains in Table cite107†2 and reinforce that stage-aligned shaping and pose-grounded exploration mitigate stage hallucination on real robots.\nL242: We identified and addressed stage hallucination, where policies fool VLM evaluators without task completion. Our EvoVLA framework introduces stage-aligned rewards with triplet contrastive learning and temporal smoothing, long-horizon memory that stabilizes intrinsic shaping over extended interactions, and pose-based exploration, which grounds curiosity in the relative object-gripper pose space. These components work synergistically to prevent visual shortcuts and substantially reduce spurious exploration.\nL351: where $\\xi=(o_{0},a_{0},\\tilde{r}_{0},\\ldots)$ is a trajectory, $H$ is the episode horizon (400 steps in our DISCOVERSE experiments), $\\gamma=0.995$ is the discount factor (standard for long-horizon manipulation), and $\\tilde{r}_{t}$ is the combined reward from the main paper, which balances sparse task-success signal ($r^{e}_{t}$), dense stage-aligned feedback from SAR ($r^{\\text{stage}}_{t}$), and exploration bonuses from POE ($r^{\\text{cur}}_{t}$, $r^{\\text{prog}}_{t}$) with fixed weight $\\rho=0.6$.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLlamazip: Leveraging LLaMA for Lossless Text Compression and Training Dataset Detection (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.17589v1)\nciteturn6744view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn6742view2\",\"pattern\":\"decompress\"}); Total lines: 406\nL61: Lossless compression is a trade-off between savings on data storage and bandwidth, since compressed data require less space, and the computing time needed to recover the data, since instead of merely accessing the data it is first necessary to decompress it. However, modern hardware and software algorithms are highly efficient, and the decompression time is often negligible compared to the benefits.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLlamazip: Leveraging LLaMA for Lossless Text Compression and Training Dataset Detection (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.17589v1)\nciteturn6744view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn6742view2\",\"pattern\":\"membership\"}); Total lines: 406\nNo matching text found for \"membership\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTime dependent loss reweighting for flow matching and diffusion models is theoretically justified (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.16599v1)\nciteturn6744view6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn6742view3\",\"pattern\":\"Assumption\"}); Total lines: 814\nL120: For Bregman divergences with $\\widehat{\\Omega}_{t,x}=\\mathrm{ri}(\\Omega_{t,x})$, a closure property is required that, in practice, is nonrestrictive (cf. Assumption cite43†3 and its succeeding remark). Note that whenever $\\Omega_{t,x}=V_{t,x}$, so that the loss function is defined on the whole space, types (i) and (ii) coincide.\nL144: The third condition is automatically true if $\\widehat{\\Omega}_{t,x}=\\Omega_{t,x}$, since $\\Omega_{t,x}$ is convex and closed. For $\\widehat{\\Omega}_{t,x}=\\mathrm{ri}(\\Omega_{t,x})$, the condition is not particularly restrictive. Indeed, if it does not hold, then the essential convex hull of $z\\mapsto F_{t}^{z}(x)$ lies in the boundary of $\\Omega_{t,x}$, as noted in the case when $\\mathcal{Z}$ is finite in cite38†Banerjee et al. (2005) .\nL145: If this condition doesn’t hold exactly, then you can only ever be able to approach the true expectation, and this assumption is implicit in e.g. parametrizing probabilities via a sigmoid function in discrete models — in which case, the model cannot ever emit a probability of exactly $0$ or $1$ with finite logits.\nL146: ###### Remark 4.3.\nL147: \nL148: The condition that $\\mathcal{D}$ dominates $\\lambda$, i.e., $\\mathcal{D}\\gg\\lambda$, is equivalent to the condition that for any $A\\subset[0,1]$ with $\\lambda(A)>0$, it also holds $\\mathcal{D}(A)>0$. In other words, the time distribution must assign positive mass to any subset of $[0,1]$ with positive Lebesgue measure. Equivalently, we could have said that $\\lambda$ is absolutely continuous with respect to $\\mathcal{D}$.\nL199: which shows that $\\int_{A}w(t)\\mathcal{D}(dt)>0$ whenever $\\lambda(A)>0$. Let $K:=\\int_{[0,1]}w(t)\\mathcal{D}(dt)$. Then since $\\lambda([0,1])=1>0$, it follows that $K>0$. Also note that $K<\\infty$, since by assumption $\\int w(t)\\mathcal{D}(dt)=\\mathbb{E}_{t\\sim\\mathcal{D}}[w(t)]<\\infty.$ Hence,\nL200: \nL201:  | $$\\widetilde{\\mathcal{D}}(A)=K^{-1}\\int_{A}w(t)\\mathcal{D}(dt)>0$$  |\nL202: \nL203: for all $A\\subset[0,1]$ with $\\lambda(A)>0$. Hence, $\\widetilde{D}\\gg\\lambda$, and we conclude by noting that\nL248:  | $\\displaystyle\\langle p_{t},\\mathcal{L}_{t}^{\\theta}f\\rangle$  | $\\displaystyle=\\langle p_{t},\\mathcal{L}_{t}f\\rangle$  |\nL249:  |  | $\\displaystyle=\\partial_{t}\\langle p_{t},f\\rangle$  |\nL250: \nL251: which by Assumption cite49†5 implies that $X_{1}^{\\theta}\\sim p_{1}(dx)$. ∎\nL252: ###### Example 4.9.\nL253: Suppose $\\mathcal{D}$ has a density against the Lebesgue measure that is positive Lebesgue-almost everywhere in $[0,1]$. That is, it would permissible if its density was zero on a set of Lebesgue-measure zero. Then $\\mathcal{D}\\gg\\lambda$ since $A\\subset[0,1]$ and $\\lambda(A)>0$ implies $\\mathcal{D}(A)>0$, so we may take our loss to be weighted by $\\mathcal{D}$.\nL290: where we have used Lemma cite50†D.1 in the third equality, possible by Assumption cite51†1 in Section cite10†4.1 . This shows that\nL291: \nL292:  | $$\\mathcal{L}_{t}f(x):=\\langle\\mathcal{K}_{t,x}f(x),\\ F_{t}(x)\\rangle_{V_{t,x}}$$  |\nL293: \nL294: solves the KFE for $p_{t}$. We also note that $F_{t}(x)\\in\\Omega_{t,x}$, since $\\Omega_{t,x}$ is convex and closed and $F_{t}(x)$ is the limit of convex combinations of elements in $\\Omega_{t,x}$. ∎\nL458: ###### Remark 4.18.\nL459: \nL460: In accordance with Assumption cite58†2 in Appendix cite21†C , the expected number of jumps should be finite. This corresponds to\nL461: \nL462:  | $$\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\int_{0}^{1}\\lambda_{\\mathrm{total}}(t,X_{t})dt\\right]<\\infty$$  |\nL463: \nL464: in the above.\nL715: ###### Proof.\nL716: \nL717: First we show that $t\\mapsto\\langle p_{t},g\\rangle$ is continuous for all $g\\in C_{0}(S)$. By Assumption cite65†3 , there is a sequence $(g_{n})_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\subset\\mathcal{T}$ such that $\\|g_{n}-g\\|_{\\infty}\\to 0$ as $n\\to\\infty$, and we see that\nL718: \nL719:  | $\\displaystyle\\sup_{t\\in[0,1]}|\\langle p_{t},g_{n}\\rangle-\\langle p_{t},g\\rangle|=\\sup_{t\\in[0,1]}\\left|\\int(g_{n}(x)-g(x))p_{t}(dx)\\right|$  | $\\displaystyle\\leq\\|g_{n}-g\\|_{\\infty}$  |\nL720:  |  | $\\displaystyle\\to 0,\\text{ as }n\\to\\infty.$  |\nL725: Now, we have that $\\mathcal{L}_{t_{n}}f\\to\\mathcal{L}_{t}f$ as $n\\to\\infty$ by Assumption cite65†3 , so that\nL726: \nL727:  | $$|\\langle p_{t_{n}},\\mathcal{L}_{t_{n}}f-\\mathcal{L}_{t}f\\rangle|\\leq\\|\\mathcal{L}_{t_{n}}f-\\mathcal{L}_{t}f\\|_{\\infty}\\to 0,\\text{ as }n\\to\\infty.$$  |\nL728: \nL729: Moreover, for all $f\\in\\mathcal{T}$ it holds $\\mathcal{L}_{t}f\\in C_{0}(S)$ since $X_{t}$ is Feller by Assumption cite66†1 , so we have that\nL730:  | $$|\\langle p_{t_{n}}-p_{t},\\mathcal{L}_{t}f\\rangle|=|\\langle p_{t_{n}},\\mathcal{L}_{t}f\\rangle-\\langle p_{t},\\mathcal{L}_{t}f\\rangle|\\to 0,\\text{ as }n\\to\\infty$$  |\nL731: \nL732: since we showed the map $t\\mapsto\\langle p_{t},g\\rangle$ was continuous for all $g\\in C_{0}(S)$ (which implies that $\\langle p_{t_{n}},g\\rangle\\to\\langle p_{t},g\\rangle$ if $t_{n}\\to t$ and $g\\in\\mathcal{T}$). Combining the above, we get\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTime dependent loss reweighting for flow matching and diffusion models is theoretically justified (https://arxiv.org/html/2511.16599v1)\nciteturn6744view7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn6742view3\",\"pattern\":\"time-dependent\"}); Total lines: 814\nL15:   1. cite5†Abstract L16:   2. cite6†1 Introduction L17:   3. cite7†2 Preliminaries L18:   4. cite8†3 Previous work L19:   5. cite9†4 Result L20:     1. cite10†4.1 Time and state varying linear parametrizations L21:     2. cite11†4.2 Time and state varying loss L22:     3. cite12†4.3 Time dependent loss reweighting L23:     4. cite13†4.4 Generator Matching L24:     5. cite14†4.5 Sums of linear parametrizations L25:     6. cite15†4.6 Flow matching $X_{1}$ prediction L26:     7. cite16†4.7 Diffusion models and $x_{0}$-prediction L27:     8. cite17†4.8 Rescaling time-dependent constants out of jump models L28:     9. cite18†4.9 Edit Flows Propositions L29:   6. cite19†A Properties of Bregman Divergences L30:   7. cite20†B Examples of Bregman Divergences L31:   8. cite21†C Regularity conditions inherited from Generator Matching L32:   9. cite22†D Regularity conditions for linear parametrizations of conditional generators L33:   10. cite23†References L34: cite24†License: CC BY 4.0†info.arxiv.org L35: \nL36: arXiv:2511.16599v1 [stat.ML] 20 Nov 2025\nL37: # Time dependent loss reweighting for flow matching and diffusion models is theoretically justified\nL38: \nL39: Lukas Billera ^{†}^{†}thanks: Correspondence to: lukas.billera@ki.se and benjamin.murrell@ki.se Affiliation: Department of Microbiology, Tumor and Cell Biology, Karolinska Institutet    Hedwig Nora Nordlinder Affiliation: Department of Microbiology, Tumor and Cell Biology, Karolinska Institutet    Ben Murrell^{∗} Affiliation: Department of Microbiology, Tumor and Cell Biology, Karolinska Institutet\nL40: August 24, 2026\nL41: ###### Abstract\nL42: This brief note clarifies that, in Generator Matching (which subsumes a large family of flow matching and diffusion models over continuous, manifold, and discrete spaces), both the Bregman divergence loss and the linear parameterization of the generator can depend on both the current state $X_{t}$ and the time $t$, and we show that the expectation over time in the loss can be taken with respect to a broad class of time distributions.\nL43: We also show this for Edit Flows, which falls outside of Generator Matching. That the loss can depend on $t$ clarifies that time-dependent loss weighting schemes, often used in practice to stabilize training, are theoretically justified when the specific flow or diffusion scheme is a special case of Generator Matching (or Edit Flows). It also often simplifies the construction of $X_{1}$-predictor schemes, which are sometimes preferred for model-related reasons.\nL44: We show examples that rely upon the dependence of linear parameterizations, and of the Bregman divergence loss, on $t$ and $X_{t}$.\nL45: ## 1 Introduction\nL46: \nL47: Diffusion (cite25†Ho et al., 2020 ; cite26†Song et al., 2021b ) and Flow Matching (cite27†Lipman et al., 2023 ) approaches to generative modeling both train a model to transport samples from a simple distribution to the data distribution. This family of approaches was recently subsumed under ‘Generator Matching’, which characterizes a general space of states, processes, and losses under which conditional samples can be used to train a marginal generator.\nL48: The loss used for these models generally involves i) a term that compares the model’s prediction to some function of the conditional process generator, and ii) a time-dependent scaling constant prescribed by the specific theoretical framework used to justify the method. Since the outset of the field (e.g., cite25†Ho et al.\nL49: (2020) ) right up until recently (cite28†Nguyen et al., 2025 ) the prescribed time-dependent loss scaling is discarded or adjusted in practice, usually motivated empirically and often without theoretical justification (with exceptions, e.g. cite29†Zhang et al. (2025) ; cite30†Oresten et al. (2025) ).\nL50: With unlimited compute, if you trained a separate model for each time $t$ and state $X_{t}$, scaling each model’s loss by a different positive constant should not change what that model learns, suggesting that time and state dependent loss scaling is justified and merely a practical issue. Here we formalize this notion, and extend it in ways that are trickier to heuristically reason about.\nL51: ## 2 Preliminaries\nL52: Throughout this note, we make use of notation and definitions similar to those found in Generator Matching (GM) (cite31†Holderrieth et al., 2025 ) and Flow Matching Guide (FMG) (cite32†Lipman et al., 2024 ). Following FMG, generator matching prescribes a conditional probability path $p_{t}(dx|z)$ interpolating between a simple initial distribution $p_{0}$ and a target distribution $p_{1}$ on a state space $S$, conditioned on a latent state $z\\in\\mathcal{Z}$.\nL53: With a distribution $p_{Z}$ over $\\mathcal{Z}$, we consider the marginal probability path $p_{t}(dx)$ defined via the two-stage sampling procedure\nL54:  | $$Z\\sim p_{Z},\\ (X_{t}|Z=z)\\sim p_{t|Z}(dx|z)\\implies X_{t}\\sim p_{t}(dx).$$  |\nL73: ## 3 Previous work\nL74: \nL75: The theoretical aspects of time-dependent loss re-weightings for flow and diffusion models are discussed in (cite34†Kingma et al., 2023 ), (cite35†Song et al., 2021a ), (cite32†Lipman et al., 2024 ), (cite36†Esser et al., 2024 ), and others. We do not attempt an exhaustive survey here and instead try to clarify this in a general setting.\nL76: \nL77: ## 4 Result\nL156: ###### Example 4.6.\nL157: \nL158: The density could, for example, be zero on either or both sides of the boundary, as for a $\\mathrm{Beta}(\\alpha,\\beta)$ distribution.\nL159: \nL160: ### 4.3 Time dependent loss reweighting\nL161: \nL162: Note: In what follows, as in GM, we denote\nL163: \nL164:  | $$\\langle\\mu,f\\rangle=\\int f(x)\\mu(dx)$$  |\nL165: \nL166: for the duality pairing between probability measures $\\mu$ on $S$ and test functions $f\\in\\mathcal{T}$.\nL340: ### 4.6 Flow matching $X_{1}$ prediction\nL341: \nL342: The endpoint prediction formulation of flow matching losses provides a natural way to obtain a time-dependent linear parameterization of the drift, and is discussed in e.g. cite29†Zhang et al. (2025) ; cite32†Lipman et al. (2024) ; cite30†Oresten et al. (2025) , and others.\nL343: In flow matching, conditioned on terminating at the endpoint $x_{1}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n}$, a time-dependent vector field $u_{t}(x|x_{1})$ governs the drift along the conditional paths. Suppose that $u_{t}(x|x_{1})$ is affine in $x_{1}$, i.e.\nL344: \nL345:  | $$u_{t}(x|x_{1})=A_{t,x}x_{1}+b_{t,x},$$  |\nL346: \nL347: with $A_{t,x}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n\\times n}$ and $b_{t,x}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{n}$. We write the infinitesimal generator corresponding to this vector field as\nL396: ###### Example 4.16.\nL397: \nL398: Suppose that $u_{t}(x|x_{0}):=\\nabla_{x}\\log p_{t}(x|x_{0})=A_{t,x}x_{0}+b_{t,x}$ is affine in $x_{0}$. Then a valid CGM loss is given by\nL399: \nL400:  | $$L(\\theta)=\\mathbb{E}_{t\\sim\\mathcal{D},x_{0}\\sim p_{\\mathrm{data}},x_{t}\\sim p_{t}(\\cdot|x_{0})}[w(t)\\|x_{0}-\\hat{x}_{0}^{\\theta}(t,x_{t})\\|^{2}]$$  |\nL401: \nL402: for a reweighting function $w(t)$ that is positive $\\lambda$-almost everywhere on $[0,1]$.\nL403: ### 4.8 Rescaling time-dependent constants out of jump models\nL404: As in GM, we let $Q_{t}(dy;x)$ specify a time-dependent jump kernel on the state space $S$. It can be useful to model jump intensities in terms of continuous hazards $h_{t,j}(x)>0$, together with individual rate multipliers $R_{t,j}(x)\\geq 0$ that are continuous, and to train a model to match the rate multiplier (cf. Equation 28 and Proposition 5 in cite56†Gat et al. (2024) , where in their notation this is done by predicting the posterior $\\hat{w}_{t}^{j}(x^{i}|X_{t})$ and the hazards are $a_{t}^{i,j}$).\nL405: Fix a time $t$ and $x\\in S$. As in Branching Flows (cite57†Nordlinder et al., 2025 ), consider a time-dependent jump kernel of the form:\nL406: \nL407:  | $$Q_{t}(dy;x)=\\sum_{j=1}^{N_{t,x}}h_{t,j}(x)R_{t,j}(x)\\delta_{\\Gamma_{t,j}(x)}(dy),$$  |\nL408: \nL409: for continuous functions $\\Gamma_{t,j}:S\\to S$ that we call jump targets, and continuous rate multipliers $R_{t,j}(x)\\geq 0$, for $j=1,\\ldots,N_{t,x}$. We define\nL410: \nL411:  | $$\\lambda_{\\mathrm{total}}(t,x)=\\int_{S}Q_{t}(dy;x)=\\sum_{j=1}^{N_{t,x}}h_{t,j}(x)R_{t,j}(x)$$  |\nL412: for each $x\\in S$ and $t\\in[0,1]$, and we require that $\\lambda_{\\mathrm{total}}(t,x)<\\infty$. The associated process $X_{t}$ can be described by:\nL413: \nL414:  | $$X_{t+\\Delta t}=\\begin{cases}X_{t}&\\quad\\text{with probability}\\quad 1-\\Delta t\\lambda_{\\mathrm{total}}(t,x)+o(\\Delta t)\\\\\nL415: \\sim J_{t}(dy;x)&\\quad\\text{with probability}\\quad\\Delta t\\lambda_{\\mathrm{total}}(t,x)+o(\\Delta t),\\end{cases}$$  |\nL416: \nL417: where in the above, the distribution $J_{t}(dy;x)$ is the normalized jump distribution:\nL418:  | $$J_{t}(dy;x)=\\frac{Q_{t}(dy;x)}{\\lambda_{\\mathrm{total}}(t,x)}=\\frac{1}{\\lambda_{\\mathrm{total}}(t,x)}\\sum_{j=1}^{N_{t,x}}h_{t,j}(x)R_{t,j}(x)\\delta_{\\Gamma_{t,j}(x)}(dy)$$  |\nL419: \nL420: having infinitesimal generator\nL421: \nL422:  | $$\\mathcal{L}_{t}f(x)=\\int(f(y)-f(x))Q_{t}(dy;x)=\\sum_{j=1}^{N_{t,x}}(f(\\Gamma_{t,j}(x))-f(x))h_{t,j}(x)R_{t,j}(x).$$  |\nL423: \nL424: Write\nL425: \nL426:  | $$R_{t}(x)=(R_{t,j}(x))_{j=1}^{N_{t,x}}.$$  |\nL427: \nL428: A time and state dependent linear parametrization of the generator is given by\nL429:  | $$\\mathcal{L}_{t}f(x)=\\langle\\mathcal{K}_{t}f(x),R_{t}(x)\\rangle_{V_{t,x}},$$  |\nL430: \nL431: where $V_{t,x}=\\mathbb{R}^{N_{t,x}}$ is equipped with the inner product\nL432: \nL433:  | $$\\langle v,w\\rangle_{V_{t,x}}=\\sum_{j=1}^{N_{t,x}}h_{t,j}(x)v^{j}w^{j},$$  |\nL434: \nL435: and the linear map $\\mathcal{K}_{t,x}:\\mathcal{T}\\to\\mathbb{R}^{N_{t,x}}$ sends\nL436: \nL437:  | $$\\mathcal{K}_{t,x}f=(f(\\Gamma_{t,i}(x))-f(x))_{i=1}^{N_{t,x}}.$$  |\nL438: ###### Example 4.17.\nL439: \nL440: In accordance with the above, for latent $z\\in\\mathcal{Z}$ we specify a conditional atomic time-dependent jump kernel through conditional rate multipliers $R_{t,j}^{z}(x)$:\nL441: \nL442:  | $$Q_{t}^{z}(dy;x)=\\sum_{j=1}^{N_{t,x}}h_{t,j}(x)R_{t,j}^{z}(x)\\delta_{\\Gamma_{t,j}(x)}(dy),$$  |\n"
}
